So, we definitely need a special "initialize that box" intrinsic. It's entirely impossible to get the same behavior with more reasonable, smaller-scoped operations without introducing extra copies or extra blocks/scopes and while ensuring the uninitialized box gets dropped if the expression panics (or returns).
The only option I can still think of is to have the intrinsic generate a method call to convert &mut Box<MaybeUninit<T>> into *mut T. That would need a new lang item. Or maybe a new kind of primitive cast or so. Anything that ensures there's a lifetime relationship between the two uses of T.

{ super let $pat = $expr1; $expr2 } isn't helpful. The way we'd like to write this is roughly:
{
  let mut b = Box::new_uninit();
  let ptr = MaybeUninit::as_mut_ptr(&mut *b);
  write_via_move(ptr, [$($x),+]);
  b
}
We'd want to lifetime-expand the [$($x),+] here if they contain things like &fun().
Also note that even if lifetime expansion works, type inference also often fails for code that currently works. Feel free to experiment with this but I tried so many variants of this, I am done and just awaiting review now.

Consider this implementation:
#![allow(internal_features)]
#![feature(core_intrinsics)]
#![feature(rustc_attrs)]
#![feature(super_let)]

use std::mem::MaybeUninit;

// This function is actually safe
#[inline(always)] // Or #[rustc_force_inline]
#[rustc_nounwind]
fn write_to_box<T>(mut dst: Box<MaybeUninit<T>>, src: T) -> Box<T> {
    let x: &mut MaybeUninit<T> = &mut *dst;
    let x = x as *mut MaybeUninit<T> as *mut T;
    unsafe { core::intrinsics::write_via_move(x, src) };
    unsafe { dst.assume_init() }
}

macro_rules! my_vec {
    [$($x:expr),+ $(,)?] => {
        {
            let x = Box::new_uninit();
            super let x = (write_to_box(x, [$($x),+]) as Box<[_]>).into_vec();
            x
        }
    }
}

